 13194139534598 true] [17592186298024 1113 #uuid "17420bc3-cd32-5b9b-bcee-d0c6c81b8d71" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298024 1145 "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3\\sqrt{xy} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ | $9\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298025 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298026 13194139534598 true] [17592186298025 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534598 true] [17592186298025 1041 "field-1" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298025 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298026 13194139534598 true] [17592186298025 1110 #uuid "cda0fe82-85d9-5c48-94d1-8a648ddbc9d8" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298026 1034 "\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298026 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534598 true] [17592186298026 1111 #uuid "2c09665f-eeed-54bf-9cf6-aa26d3b7b748" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298027 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298028 13194139534598 true] [17592186298027 1089 "q-351673" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298027 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534598 true] [17592186298027 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$y(3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298027 1113 #uuid "c99ae56b-4149-54c1-8619-9d86d9094795" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298027 1145 "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{0}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{1}=e \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=e\\cdot 1=e \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $e$ | $e$ |\n| $2$ | $1+e$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx={e}^{2}\\cdot 1={e}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=(1+e)+{e}^{2}=1+e+{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $e$ | $e$ |\n| $2$ | $1+e$ | ${e}^{2}$ | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $1+e+{e}^{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈1+e+{e}^{2}.$" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298028 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298029 13194139534598 true] [17592186298028 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298030 13194139534598 true] [17592186298028 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534598 true] [17592186298028 1041 "field-1" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298028 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298030 13194139534598 true] [17592186298028 1110 #uuid "ac1b7429-79d0-5eb1-97e5-adca3823afa5" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298029 1034 "3" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298029 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534598 true] [17592186298029 1111 #uuid "9df77caf-9766-52db-b28d-6d7a8c0d3869" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298030 1034 "1+e+e^2" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534598 true] [17592186298030 1111 #uuid "93949281-e59f-55e7-9a19-f44389ee7540" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298031 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298032 13194139534598 true] [17592186298031 1089 "q-53858" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298031 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534598 true] [17592186298031 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298031 1113 #uuid "61c1b09e-425b-5c97-b1c1-c9772c7f5a2f" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298031 1145 "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈4.$" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298033 13194139534598 true] [17592186298032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298034 13194139534598 true] [17592186298032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298035 13194139534598 true] [17592186298032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298036 13194139534598 true] [17592186298032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298037 13194139534598 true] [17592186298032 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534598 true] [17592186298032 1041 "selection" 13194139534598 true] [17592186298032 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298037 13194139534598 true